<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alptakin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 970s</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Aftakin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alptakin was a Turkish military commander who had served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> before seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 975 and resisting the Fatimid advance into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>, he was defeated by the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> in 978 and captured. Al-Aziz pardoned him and took him into his service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, a sign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>' growing reliance on Turkish soldiers.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alptakin, also called Aftakin, was a freedman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla Bakhtiyar</a>, the Buyid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Nothing more is known of him until 973, when he joined the rebellion of the Turkish officer Sabuktakin, who took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and much of Iraq and then besieged Izz al-Dawla in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a>. When Sabuktakin died during the siege, Alptakin was chosen to lead the Turks. A Buyid army under Izz al-Dawla's cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> marched on Iraq and by 975 had crushed the rebels at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Diyala River</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Alptakin fled west with about 300 followers and took Hims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a>, he took the Fatimid city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, where 4,000 Fatimid troops were massacred, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. When the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> campaigned in Syria, Alptakin submitted to him but kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> from annexing the city. The Fatimid general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a> besieged Damascus from July 976 until a Qarmatian army forced him to withdraw in January 977. The allies beat Jawhar at the Yarqon River and besieged him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> until April 978, when the starving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> agreed to a treaty giving Alptakin Damascus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> in return for a nominal recognition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>.</p><p>In 978 the fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> with his brothers and Dailamite followers, whom Alptakin took into his army. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz-billah">al-Aziz Billah</a> then led a new army against him. Near Ramla Alptakin's charge broke the Fatimid left wing, but a counter-attack on his centre and right killed about 20,000 of his men.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alptakin escaped into the desert, where he nearly died of thirst, and was taken in by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mufarrij-ibn-daghfal">Mufarrij ibn Daghfal ibn al-Jarrah</a>, the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayy">Tayy</a> and an old friend, who then handed him over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aziz">al-Aziz</a> for 100,000 gold dinars. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> treated him with honour and took him and his Turkish followers into the Fatimid army. Not long afterwards he was poisoned by al-Aziz's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, Yaqub ibn Killis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alptakin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under al-Aziz, the caliphate defeated the Turkish commander <b>Alptakin</b> near Ramla in the late 970s and pressed its claims in Syria against local and Byzantine-backed rivals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
